    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<p>A <strong>compact under desk elliptical</strong> is built for the reality of most home offices: a small desk, a shallow footwell, maybe a drawer unit or crossbar in the way, and nowhere to store bulky equipment. The best compact models keep a low profile and a short footprint without giving up the smooth, quiet motion that makes seated pedaling worth doing.</p>
<p>For most small desks, the Cubii JR2 is the best balance of size, quiet and build quality. If every inch counts, the Cubii GO is the smallest Cubii. Below are six picks based on specs and owner reports, plus the two measurements that decide whether any machine will actually fit.</p>

<h2>Best Compact Under Desk Ellipticals at a Glance</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Best overall compact:</strong> [[amazon:cubii-jr2]]</li>
<li><strong>Smallest footprint:</strong> [[amazon:cubii-go]]</li>
<li><strong>Best budget compact:</strong> [[amazon:jisuo|JISUO under desk elliptical]]</li>
<li><strong>Best value from a known brand:</strong> [[amazon:sunny-sf-e3872|Sunny Health &amp; Fitness SF-E3872]]</li>
<li><strong>Best compact and lightweight:</strong> [[amazon:cubii-move]]</li>
<li><strong>Best compact for standing:</strong> [[amazon:stamina-inmotion-e1000|Stamina InMotion E1000 compact strider]]</li>
</ol>

<h2>Compact, Mini and Portable: What the Labels Mean</h2>
<p>Listings use these words loosely, so it helps to know what you are actually shopping for.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Compact</strong> usually means a full seated elliptical with a smaller footprint and lower profile than average. You keep a proper stride and resistance range.</li>
<li><strong>Mini</strong> often means a smaller, simpler machine with a shorter stride and lighter resistance. Fine for gentle movement, less so for a workout. See our <a href="/mini-elliptical-under-desk/">mini elliptical under desk</a> guide.</li>
<li><strong>Portable</strong> is about weight and handling: a carry handle and a body light enough to move daily.</li>
</ul>
<p>The best compact under desk elliptical for you balances all three. If it will stay under your desk, prioritize a low profile and a stable base. If it will move every day, weight matters more than size.</p>

<h2>Will It Fit? Measure Before You Buy a Compact Under Desk Elliptical</h2>
<p>Small spaces cause two different fit problems, and a "compact" label only solves one of them.</p>
<h3>1. Floor space (footprint)</h3>
<p>Measure the depth of your footwell from the front edge of the desk to the back panel or wall, and the width between desk legs or drawer units. Then sit in your chair at your normal distance and see how much floor is actually free in front of your feet. Compare that with the machine's listed length and width, and leave a little room so the pedals do not hit anything at the back of the stride.</p>
<h3>2. Knee clearance (height)</h3>
<p>This is the one that catches people out. Your knee rises at the top of every stride. If your desk is low, or has a keyboard tray or drawer, your knee can hit it even if the machine fits on the floor perfectly. Small desks often have a drawer right where your knees go.</p>
<p>Enter your measurements below to see whether you have enough room.</p>
[[tool:clearance]]
<div class="callout"><p><strong>Tip:</strong> if clearance is tight, lowering your chair slightly or sitting a little further back usually helps more than switching machines. A chair without a thick seat cushion buys you room too.</p></div>

<h2>The Best Compact Under Desk Ellipticals, Reviewed</h2>
<h3>1. Cubii JR2: Best Overall Compact</h3>
<p>The JR2 is compact without feeling cramped. It slides under most desks, keeps a low profile so knees stay clear, and its magnetic resistance is quiet enough for a studio apartment or a shared room. It is heavier than mini models, which helps it stay put but makes it less convenient to store between sessions. If it can live under your desk full time, it is the one to get.</p>
[[product:cubii-jr2|1]]

<h3>2. Cubii GO: Smallest Footprint</h3>
<p>The Cubii GO is the most portable Cubii, with a small footprint and a built-in display. It is easy to move and tuck away, which makes it ideal if your desk doubles as a dining table or you work from different rooms. The tradeoffs are a shorter stride and lighter resistance than full-size models, so it suits gentle movement more than a workout. See our <a href="/portable-elliptical-under-desk/">portable under desk elliptical</a> guide for more on the GO.</p>
[[product:cubii-go|2]]

<h3>3. JISUO: Best Budget Compact</h3>
<p>The JISUO is a compact pick for small desks with easy setup and a budget-friendly price. Its resistance range is limited and the display is basic, but if you mainly want light movement in a tiny space, owners say it does the job. Read our <a href="/jisuo-under-desk-elliptical-review/">JISUO under desk elliptical review</a>. [[button:jisuo]]</p>

<h3>4. Sunny SF-E3872: Best Value From a Known Brand</h3>
<p>The Sunny is compact and easy to store, with adjustable resistance from an established fitness brand. It is less polished than a Cubii, but it gives you the core experience in a small package and is a sensible first machine. [[button:sunny-sf-e3872]]</p>

<h3>5. Cubii Move: Best Compact and Lightweight</h3>
<p>If you will carry your machine between rooms or store it every evening, the Cubii Move is light enough to lift with one hand and keeps Cubii's quiet ride. It slides more on smooth floors, so use a mat. Our <a href="/lightweight-under-desk-elliptical/">lightweight under desk elliptical</a> guide covers that tradeoff in detail. [[button:cubii-move]]</p>

<h3>6. Stamina InMotion E1000: Best Compact for Standing</h3>
<p>Many people searching for an InMotion compact elliptical want something they can stand on. The Stamina InMotion E1000 is a compact strider with a small footprint that is built to be used standing and also works seated. It is noisier than magnetic seated models and you need a stable surface to hold for balance. See our <a href="/stamina-inmotion-review/">Stamina InMotion review</a>. [[button:stamina-inmotion-e1000]]</p>

<h2>Compact Under Desk Ellipticals Compared</h2>
[[compare:cubii-jr2,cubii-go,jisuo,sunny-sf-e3872,cubii-move,stamina-inmotion-e1000]]

<h2>Compact Elliptical vs Other Small Space Exercise Equipment</h2>
<p>If you are shopping for small space exercise equipment in general, here is how a compact seated elliptical stacks up against the usual alternatives.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Option</th><th>Space needed</th><th>Needs a standing desk?</th><th>Good for</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Compact seated elliptical</td><td>Fits in your existing footwell</td><td>No</td><td>Movement while you work, low impact</td></tr>
<tr><td>Under desk bike</td><td>Similar footprint, more knee height</td><td>No</td><td>Stronger resistance</td></tr>
<tr><td>Walking pad</td><td>A strip of floor plus storage room</td><td>Yes</td><td>Real walking steps</td></tr>
<tr><td>Compact standing strider</td><td>Small footprint, open space above</td><td>For standing use</td><td>Standing cardio</td></tr>
<tr><td>Full-size compact home elliptical</td><td>A dedicated corner</td><td>No desk at all</td><td>Full-body workouts</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>A compact home elliptical with handles gives a bigger workout but takes a corner of the room and cannot be used while you type. If you want to combine work and movement, the seated machine wins on space every time. Compare the trade-offs in <a href="/under-desk-elliptical-vs-walking-pad/">under desk elliptical vs walking pad</a>.</p>

<h2>Small Desk Setups and What Works in Each</h2>
<h3>Writing desk or narrow console desk</h3>
<p>These are often shallow, so footprint is the limit. Choose the Cubii GO or another short-base model, and sit slightly further back than usual to give the pedals room.</p>
<h3>Desk with a drawer pedestal</h3>
<p>A drawer unit on one side narrows the footwell. Measure the gap between the pedestal and the other leg, and center the machine in front of your chair rather than the desk.</p>
<h3>Desk with a center drawer or keyboard tray</h3>
<p>This is a clearance problem, not a footprint one. Pick a low-profile model such as the JR2, lower your chair, or remove the tray. Run the clearance checker above with the drawer height, not the desktop height.</p>
<h3>Dining table as a desk</h3>
<p>Tables are often higher than desks, which helps clearance, but the machine has to be put away at mealtimes. A lighter model like the Cubii Move or GO makes that a ten-second job.</p>
<h3>Corner or L-shaped desk</h3>
<p>Sit at the long side where your legs have the most room. In the corner itself, the angled footwell rarely gives a straight line for the pedals.</p>

<h2>What to Look For in a Compact Elliptical for Small Spaces</h2>
<h3>Low pedal height</h3>
<p>Small desks are often lower or have drawers. A low pedal path keeps your knees down. This matters more than overall size.</p>
<h3>Short, stable base</h3>
<p>A shorter base fits shallow footwells, but it should still be wide enough not to rock. Rubber feet help on hard floors.</p>
<h3>Quiet magnetic resistance</h3>
<p>In a small apartment, sound and vibration have nowhere to go. Magnetic resistance and a mat keep things peaceful. Our <a href="/quietest-under-desk-elliptical/">quietest under desk elliptical</a> guide lists the quietest options.</p>
<h3>Carry handle and storage shape</h3>
<p>If the machine will not live under the desk, look for a carry handle and a shape that stands on end or slides under a bed or sofa.</p>
<h3>Manual over motorized</h3>
<p>Motorized units are usually bulkier and need an outlet nearby, which is harder to arrange in tight spaces.</p>

<h2>Making a Small Desk Work</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Clear the footwell.</strong> Move power strips, bins and bags. Cables near the pedals are a tripping and snagging risk.</li>
<li><strong>Use a [[amazon:cubii-mat|non-slip mat]].</strong> In a tight space, a machine that creeps forward hits the back panel and makes noise.</li>
<li><strong>Lock your chair.</strong> On a small desk you have little room to roll back; [[amazon:caster-cups|caster cups]] keep you in position.</li>
<li><strong>Remove the keyboard tray if knees hit it.</strong> A tray steals the clearance you need.</li>
<li><strong>Use short sessions.</strong> Even in a cramped space, ten minutes every half hour adds up. The <a href="/tools/desk-pedal-timer/">desk pedal timer</a> can prompt you.</li>
</ol>

<h2>Can a Compact Machine Still Give You a Workout?</h2>
<p>Yes, within limits. A compact model with magnetic resistance, like the JR2 or Sunny, lets you turn the effort up enough to feel it in your legs, and the calorie math is the same as for larger seated machines: light seated pedaling sits around 2.0 METs versus about 1.3 for sitting, according to the Compendium of Physical Activities. For most people that means roughly 100 to 200 calories an hour at a light to moderate pace.</p>
<p>Mini machines with a short stride and light resistance burn less, because there is simply less work to do. If calorie burn matters, choose compact over mini. The <a href="/tools/under-desk-elliptical-calorie-calculator/">calorie calculator</a> gives you a personal estimate.</p>

<h2>Storing a Compact Elliptical Between Sessions</h2>
<p>If your machine cannot live under the desk, storage becomes part of the routine. Owners who store theirs well tend to keep using them.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Pick one spot</strong> close to the desk, such as beside a bookcase or under a bed, so getting it out takes seconds.</li>
<li><strong>Lift with your legs</strong> and use the carry handle where there is one. Heavier models are easier to slide on their mat than to carry.</li>
<li><strong>Keep the mat with it.</strong> Rolling the machine out onto its mat each time saves your floor.</li>
<li><strong>Check the hardware occasionally,</strong> since moving a machine daily can loosen bolts faster.</li>
</ul>
<p>Honestly, though, a machine that stays under the desk gets used more. If you can spare the footwell, leave it there.</p>

<h2>Who Should Buy a Compact Under Desk Elliptical</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Apartment and studio dwellers</strong> who have no room for a treadmill or full elliptical.</li>
<li><strong>Remote workers with small desks</strong> or a desk tucked into a bedroom or living room corner.</li>
<li><strong>Seniors</strong> who want a low, stable machine in front of an armchair. The best compact elliptical for seniors is one that is low and steady, like the JR2.</li>
<li><strong>Students</strong> in dorm rooms with limited floor space.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Skip it if</strong> you want a full-body workout or plan to stand, unless you choose a strider built for that.</p>

<h2>Our Verdict</h2>
<p>The [[amazon:cubii-jr2]] is the best compact under desk elliptical for most small desks: low, quiet and stable. Go smaller with the Cubii GO if space is extremely tight, save money with the JISUO or Sunny, choose the Cubii Move if you will carry it often, and pick the Stamina InMotion if you want to stand. Whatever you choose, measure your footwell and knee clearance first. For more options, see our list of the <a href="/what-is-the-best-under-desk-elliptical/">best under desk ellipticals</a>.</p>
